const LX_B23_P1 = [
{title:"Radio interview with a festival historian",intro:"You will hear part of a radio interview with Dr Gabriela Toapanta, a historian from Latacunga who studies the Mama Negra festival.",voices:{H:V_.lew,G:V_.sar},
script:[["H","Good afternoon and welcome to Culture Corner. Every November, the streets of Latacunga fill with music, dancers and thousands of visitors for the Mama Negra festival. My guest today, Dr Gabriela Toapanta, has spent ten years studying it. Gabriela, welcome."],["G","Thanks for having me. It's my favourite subject, so you may find it hard to stop me talking!"],["H","Let's start with the origin of the festival. Where does it come from?"],["G","That's the question everyone asks, and honestly, nobody knows for sure. Most tourist leaflets will tell you it started after an eruption of Cotopaxi in the eighteenth century, when people asked the Virgin of Mercy to protect the city. That story is definitely part of it. Others say it commemorates the freeing of African slaves who worked in the area, and some historians have claimed that Spanish priests brought it over, although there's very little proof of that. My view is that several traditions, Indigenous, Spanish and African, gradually came together and turned into one festival. It's far too diverse to have a single origin."],["H","And who is the Mama Negra herself?"],["G","She's the central character, a woman with a black face who rides a horse through the streets and squirts milk and water at the crowd. People are often surprised to learn that she's always played by a man. It's a huge honour, but it's also incredibly expensive, because he pays for the food, the drinks, the costumes and the bands. Some people say it's just a way of showing off your wealth. And of course you become a bit of a celebrity in Latacunga for a year. But nobody I've interviewed does it for that. For most of them, it's a promise, something they do out of faith or gratitude."],["H","The festival has become a massive tourist attraction. Has that changed it?"],["G","In some ways, yes. There are actually two celebrations now: a small religious one in September, organised by the market women, and the big November parade. A lot of people worry that the November one is turning into a show for tourists, and some say the September one is the only genuine one. I understand that, but I'm of the opinion that a tradition has to change if it wants to survive. What really worries me is the alcohol. Drinks are offered to everyone, even teenagers, and every year there are more accidents. If nothing is done about it, the festival will end up with a bad reputation."],["H","Your research involved talking to a lot of local people. What did you learn?"],["G","I'd expected them to talk about history and politics, because that's what I'd read about in books. Instead, they talked about their grandparents. One woman told me she'd danced in the parade every year since she was six, because her grandmother used to take her, and now she brings along her own granddaughter. For many families, it's a way of staying connected to their ancestors. That's what keeps it alive, more than the tourists or the city council."],["H","Finally, what would you say to someone going for the first time?"],["G","Well, people always say book your hotel early, and that's true, they fill up months ahead. Others will tell you to buy a seat in the stands, but you'd better not spend the whole day sitting down. My main advice is to get as close to the parade as you can, and keep your eyes peeled, because the Mama Negra might soak you with milk! You can try the local food afterwards if you're still hungry."],["H","Gabriela Toapanta, thank you."]],
q:[["What does Gabriela believe about the origin of the festival?","It developed from a mix of different traditions.","It began after an eruption of Cotopaxi.","It commemorates the freeing of African slaves.","It was brought to Latacunga by Spanish priests."],["According to Gabriela, why do most men agree to play the Mama Negra?","to express their faith or gratitude","to show off their wealth","to become famous in the city","because their families have saved for years"],["What worries Gabriela most about the festival today?","the amount of alcohol that is drunk","the fact that it is becoming a show for tourists","the small size of the September celebration","the loss of its genuine religious meaning"],["What did Gabriela learn from interviewing local people?","The festival connects families across generations.","Most people are interested in its political history.","The city council keeps the tradition alive.","Many grandparents no longer take part."],["What is Gabriela's main advice for first-time visitors?","to get close to the parade","to book a hotel months in advance","to buy a seat in the stands","to try the local food"]]},
{title:"Radio interview with a fact-checker",intro:"You will hear part of a radio interview with Tomás Herrera, a journalist who works for a fact-checking website in Quito.",voices:{H:V_.hea,G:V_.fab},
script:[["H","Hello and welcome to Media Matters. These days it's almost impossible to scroll through your newsfeed without coming across a story that turns out to be false. My guest, Tomás Herrera, spends his working day checking them. Tomás, welcome."],["G","Thank you. Yes, I'm never short of work, unfortunately."],["H","How did you become a fact-checker?"],["G","By accident, really. I trained as a sports journalist and I was perfectly happy writing about football. Then, during an election campaign, my uncle sent our family group chat a video claiming that thousands of votes had been destroyed. It looked convincing, and half my relatives believed it. I spent a whole weekend tracing where it came from and discovered it had been filmed in another country, years earlier. I wrote a short article about it, which was shared far more than anything I'd ever written about football, and the following month a fact-checking website offered me a job."],["H","What kind of stories do you check most often?"],["G","People imagine it's mostly politics, and there's certainly plenty of that around elections. Health rumours come up a lot too, especially miracle cures. But the most common thing we see, week in, week out, is fake prize competitions. You know: 'This supermarket is giving away a free fridge, just click here.' They seem harmless, but they're designed to collect your personal data, so they can do real damage."],["H","How do you actually verify a story?"],["G","There's no magic machine, if that's what people think. We do use some online tools, for example to see where a photo first appeared. But the most important part of the process is old-fashioned: we pick up the phone. If a post says a hospital has run out of medicine, we call the hospital. If it claims a minister said something, we contact the minister's office. It's time-consuming, but nothing replaces talking to the people involved."],["H","Why do you think people fall for fake news so easily?"],["G","It's tempting to say people are just not very clever, but that isn't fair. I've seen professors and doctors share stories that were obviously false. If you ask me, the problem is emotional. A fake story is usually designed to make you angry or frightened, and when you're angry, you share first and think later. So my advice is simple: if a post makes you furious, that's exactly the moment you should stop and check."],["H","Do you think things will get worse with artificial intelligence?"],["G","Many of my colleagues are pessimistic. They say that soon nobody will be able to tell a real video from a fake one, and yes, the fakes are getting better. But I'm more optimistic than they are. When I started, I thought my job was to debunk every false story, which is impossible. Now I think the real job is teaching people the habit of checking. We visit schools in Quito and Latacunga every month, and the teenagers are often far more suspicious of what they see online than their parents are. If that habit spreads, the technology matters much less."],["H","Tomás Herrera, thank you."]],
q:[["What led Tomás to become a fact-checker?","He investigated a false video that his family had received.","He was tired of writing about sport.","A website asked him to report on an election.","His uncle encouraged him to change careers."],["Which type of false story does Tomás check most often?","competitions that are used to collect personal information","political stories during elections","rumours about miracle cures","old videos from other countries"],["According to Tomás, what is the most important part of checking a story?","speaking directly to the people involved","using online tools to trace photos","using a special computer programme","asking colleagues for their opinion"],["Why does Tomás think people share false stories?","Strong emotions stop them from thinking carefully.","They are not intelligent enough to notice.","They trust what professors and doctors say.","They do not have time to check them."],["How does Tomás feel about the future?","hopeful, because young people are learning to check information","worried that fake videos will be impossible to detect","confident that every false story can be debunked","as pessimistic as most of his colleagues"]]},
{title:"Radio interview with a money blogger",intro:"You will hear part of a radio interview with Carolina Endara, who writes a popular blog about money for young people in Ecuador.",voices:{H:V_.mic,G:V_.isa},
script:[["H","Welcome back to Money Talks. My guest today is Carolina Endara, whose blog, Frugal and Happy, gives money advice to students and young professionals. Carolina, welcome."],["G","Hi, thanks for inviting me."],["H","You weren't always good with money, were you?"],["G","Not at all! When I got my first job after university, I thought I was rich. I ate out every night, bought clothes I never wore and blew my budget every single month. I wasn't in serious debt, luckily, but I had absolutely nothing saved. The turning point came when my laptop broke and I had to borrow money from my younger brother to replace it. He was still at school! It was so embarrassing that I decided to change things that same week."],["H","What's the first thing you tell people who want to start budgeting?"],["G","Most people expect me to say 'make a spreadsheet', and spreadsheets are great, but that's not where I'd begin. Others think you should cut out all treats immediately, which never works; you just give up after two weeks. What I always suggest is spending one month writing down every single thing you buy, even a coffee or a bus ticket. Don't change anything yet, just notice. Almost everyone is shocked by how much goes on small things. After that, you can start putting money aside."],["H","Many young people say they simply don't earn enough to save. What would you say to them?"],["G","I'd say I understand completely. The cost of living has gone up, and salaries haven't kept up with inflation. I'm not going to pretend that skipping a coffee will make you wealthy, and I'm not telling everyone to go and find a better-paid job either. But whereas you can't always control what you earn, you can usually control some of what you spend. And what really matters isn't the amount, it's the habit. Someone who puts aside five dollars a week at twenty-two is in a much better position than someone who waits until they earn more."],["H","Your blog is called Frugal and Happy. Is it really possible to be both?"],["G","I think so, but people often misunderstand what frugal means. It doesn't mean never spending. I've never been one for expensive gadgets, but I happily spend money on travel and on seeing my friends, because those are the things I care about. My trip to the Galápagos last year took a whole year of saving, and I don't regret a single cent."],["H","Finally, what's the biggest mistake you see?"],["G","Lending money to friends is a tricky one, and lots of readers write to me about friends who never paid them back. Credit cards cause problems too, of course. But the mistake I see most is people comparing themselves to others on social media. You see a friend's new car or holiday, and you feel you have to keep up. What you don't see is that many of them are in debt. If only people realised how much of it is borrowed money, they'd feel a lot less pressure."],["H","Carolina Endara, thank you."]],
q:[["What made Carolina change her attitude to money?","She had to borrow money from a younger relative.","She got into serious debt.","She lost her first job.","She realised she owned too many clothes."],["What does Carolina recommend people do first?","keep a record of everything they spend for a month","create a spreadsheet","stop buying treats","start putting money aside"],["What is Carolina's view on young people who say they can't save?","Building the habit is more important than the amount.","They should look for better-paid jobs.","Small savings will eventually make them wealthy.","Inflation is not as serious as they think."],["What does Carolina say about being frugal?","It means spending money on the things you value.","It means not spending money on travel.","It makes it difficult to see friends often.","It involves buying cheaper gadgets."],["According to Carolina, what is the most common mistake people make?","trying to keep up with what others show online","lending money to friends","using credit cards","spending too much on holidays"]]}
];

const LX_B23_P2 = [
{title:"Five people talk about happiness habits",topic:"happiness habits",who:["a university student","a nurse","an electrical engineer","a retired teacher","a taxi driver","a yoga instructor","a chef","an office worker"],
op:["I was reluctant to try it, but now I enjoy it.","It took months before I noticed any benefits.","It helps me savour ordinary moments.","Doing it with other people makes it more fulfilling.","I gave it up because it was too time-consuming.","It has made me more generous towards others.","It helped me get through a difficult period.","I do it mainly to relieve stress at work."],
sp:[["My girlfriend kept sending me articles about cold showers, and I kept ignoring them. I've never been one for suffering on purpose, and in Latacunga the water at six in the morning is absolutely freezing. In the end she bet me I couldn't last a week. The first day I was literally shouting. But by the third day I noticed this amazing rush afterwards, and now, two years later, I actually look forward to it. It doesn't do much for my stress at work, to be honest, but it's the best start to the day.",2,0,"dan"],
["I started a gratitude journal the year my father was ill. Most evenings I came home from the hospital exhausted, and I'd write down three good things, even tiny ones, like a colleague bringing me a coffee. A lot of people at work say journals help them relax after a busy shift, but for me it was something else. On the worst days it reminded me that life wasn't only sadness. Honestly, I'm not sure how I would have coped without it. Dad's fine now, and I still write in it, just less often.",1,6,"hea"],
["For years I walked alone in the hills above Cuenca, and I enjoyed it, but it was just exercise really. Then, after I stopped working, a neighbour invited me to join a walking group of retired people. We meet every Tuesday, we take turns bringing food, and we talk the whole way. Some of them were strangers at first, and now they're among my closest friends. The views are the same as before, but sharing them has turned a simple walk into the highlight of my week. I'd never go back to walking on my own.",3,3,"lil"],
["Everyone at the office kept telling me meditation would relieve my stress, so I downloaded an app and promised myself twenty minutes every morning. And for a few weeks it was lovely; I did feel calmer. The problem was finding the time. With two kids to get ready for school and a forty-minute drive, those twenty minutes simply didn't exist. I tried doing it at night, but I always fell asleep. I wish I'd been able to stick at it, but in the end something had to go, and it was the meditation.",7,4,"mic"],
["In a busy kitchen you eat standing up, in about three minutes, and you don't taste anything. A while ago I decided that, once a day, I'd sit down and eat one meal slowly, with no phone and no television. It sounds silly, but it's changed a lot. I notice the smell of the bread, the sound of the rain, the way the light comes through the window. Those little things were always there; I just never paid attention. I can't say it's made me kinder to anyone, but my days feel richer.",6,2,"ali"]]},
{title:"Five people talk about a personal tradition",topic:"personal and family traditions",who:["a grandfather","a teenager","a pharmacist","a musician","a primary school teacher","a shop owner","a flight attendant","a farmer"],
op:["The tradition started by accident.","I used to find the tradition embarrassing.","We had to change the tradition when the family grew.","The tradition helps me keep in touch with old friends.","I'd like to pass the tradition on to my own children.","The tradition matters more to me than to the rest of my family.","The tradition commemorates someone who has died.","We celebrate in a different way from most people."],
sp:[["Every December, my dad and I make an Año Viejo doll for the thirty-first. When I was about thirteen, I hated it. He'd make me sit on the pavement outside our house in Quito, stuffing old clothes with sawdust while my friends walked past, and I wanted the ground to swallow me up. Now I'm seventeen and it's my favourite day of the year. Last year we made a massive doll of my maths teacher, which he thought was hilarious. I'm not sure I'll have kids, but I'll definitely keep doing it.",1,1,"nic"],
["Six of us went to school together in Otavalo, and when we finished, we promised to meet every June for Inti Raymi. That was fifteen years ago, and we've only missed one year. Now we live in four different countries. I'm always flying somewhere, two of the others have moved to Spain, and we hardly message each other the rest of the year. But for those few days, we dance in the square, eat far too much and catch up with each other's lives. Without that week, I think we'd have drifted apart long ago.",6,3,"emm"],
["On the second of November, the Day of the Dead, I make colada morada and guaguas de pan, the way my wife used to. She died four years ago. I take some to the cemetery and leave it by her grave, and then I share the rest with the neighbours. My grandchildren sometimes help me, though they mostly eat the bread. It's a custom most families in Ecuador keep, but for me it isn't really about the custom. It's my way of spending the day with her.",0,6,"lew"],
["Friday is pizza night in our house, and it's been that way for nine years. It wasn't planned at all. One Friday there was a power cut, the fridge was off, and the only open place was a little pizzeria at the end of our street. The kids loved eating by candlelight so much that they asked to do it again the next week. Even after the electricity came back, we kept going. The family has grown since then, but we still squeeze around the same small table.",2,0,"eri"],
["At Christmas, most families here have a big dinner at midnight on the twenty-fourth. Not us. My parents are both musicians, and they always worked on Christmas Eve, playing in churches and hotels. So instead, on the morning of the twenty-fifth, we drive up to Cotopaxi with a flask of hot chocolate and have breakfast in the cold, looking at the volcano. My friends think we're crazy. Now I play in a choir myself, so I can't imagine Christmas any other way.",3,7,"bel"]]},
{title:"Five people talk about digital life",topic:"social media and technology",who:["a software developer","a secondary school student","a photographer","a small business owner","a psychology lecturer","a journalist","a grandmother","a delivery driver"],
op:["Notifications used to control my day.","Social media has been essential for my business.","People overlook how much personal data they give away.","I find AI useful, but I don't completely trust it.","Online friendships can be as genuine as real ones.","Sponsored content is becoming harder to recognise.","Technology has helped me feel less lonely.","Social media makes people compare themselves with others."],
sp:[["When my son moved to Madrid with his family, I thought I'd hardly see my grandchildren. I'd never used a smartphone in my life. My granddaughter set one up for me before they left, and now we have a video call every Sunday. They show me their drawings, I show them my garden, and sometimes we just leave the phone on while we eat. My friends say it isn't the same as a real visit, and of course it isn't. But living on my own, it makes a huge difference. I don't feel alone the way I did at first.",6,6,"isa"],
["I write apps for a living, so I know what goes on behind the screen. People install a free torch app and happily give it access to their contacts, their location and their microphone without reading a word. They worry about hackers, but most of their information isn't stolen at all; they hand it over themselves, because they're in a hurry to start using the app. I'm not saying everyone should give up their phones. Just stop for ten seconds and ask yourself why a torch needs to know where you are.",0,2,"ada"],
["I opened a small chocolate shop in Baños three years ago, on a street with five other chocolate shops. In the first months, almost nobody came in. Then my niece suggested filming short videos of how we make the chocolate, from the cocoa beans to the finished bars. One of them was watched by thousands of people, and tourists started arriving with their phones, asking for the shop in the video. I don't really enjoy being online, to be honest, but without it, I'd have closed the shop a long time ago.",3,1,"sar"],
["Last year I realised I was checking my phone every few minutes, even in class. Every time it buzzed, I had to look, and I'd lose my concentration completely. My exam results were terrible. So I did something my parents never expected: I turned off every notification except calls. The first week was really tricky; I kept picking up my phone anyway. But now I check my messages when I decide to, maybe three times a day. I still love social media. I'm just the one in charge now, not my phone.",1,0,"fab"],
["Ten years ago, if a famous person was paid to promote something, it said so clearly, and you knew it was an advert. Now the line is much more blurred. An influencer shows you her morning routine, mentions a face cream as if she'd just discovered it, and only a tiny label, if there is one, tells you she's been paid. Even I miss it sometimes, and I've been writing about the media for fifteen years. If you ask me, we need stricter regulation, because most young people simply can't tell the difference.",5,5,"hea"]]},
{title:"Five people talk about money",topic:"money and spending",who:["a university student","a bank employee","a street vendor","a retired police officer","an economics teacher","a social worker","a freelance designer","a hotel receptionist"],
op:["I scrimped and saved to reach a goal.","Lending money to friends is always a mistake.","I regret not saving earlier in life.","Inequality is the biggest economic problem we face.","Being frugal doesn't mean being unhappy.","A high income doesn't guarantee financial security.","Donating money gives me more satisfaction than spending it.","Irregular earnings make budgeting difficult."],
sp:[["When you're a freelancer, no two months are the same. In March I might earn three times what I need, and then in April a client pays late and I earn almost nothing. For years I spent everything in the good months and panicked in the bad ones. Friends with normal jobs tell me to just stick to a budget, but how do you plan when you have no idea what's coming in? Now I pay myself a fixed amount from a separate account, which helps, but it's still a constant headache.",6,7,"mic"],
["I worked for thirty years and never really thought about the future. There was always a reason not to save: a new car, the kids' school, a holiday. My wife and I thought the pension would be enough to live on, and it isn't, not with the cost of living now. If I'd started putting aside even a little when I was young, we'd be much more comfortable today. My grandson has just started his first job, and I keep telling him not to make the same mistake.",3,2,"dan"],
["In my second year at university I decided I wanted to go on a field trip to the Galápagos with my department. It cost far more than I had. So for ten months I took my lunch from home every day, walked instead of taking taxis and sold my old guitar. My friends thought I'd become really boring, and I suppose I had. But when I was finally standing on the beach on Santa Cruz, watching sea lions, I felt it had all been worth it. It's the thing I'm proudest of.",0,0,"ali"],
["Every day I visit families in the south of Quito who don't have enough money to live on, and then I take the bus home through neighbourhoods with huge houses and private security. The distance between them is only a few kilometres, but they might as well be different countries. People talk about inflation and recession as if they affected everyone equally, but they don't; the poorest always suffer most. Until we deal with the gap between rich and poor, I don't think anything else in the economy will really improve.",5,3,"nic"],
["You'd be surprised how many customers with excellent salaries come to the bank in trouble. Doctors, managers, people earning far more than me. They earn a lot, so they borrow a lot: a bigger flat, a second car, expensive holidays. Then one thing goes wrong, an illness or a lost contract, and they can't pay. Meanwhile some of our customers with modest earnings have very healthy savings. I'm not saying rich people are careless, but I've learnt that what you earn tells me very little about how safe you are.",1,5,"emm"]]}
];

const CL_B23_1 = [
{title:"Set 1",ex:[
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about strange lights he saw in the sky.",q:"What does he think the lights probably were?",o:["drones from a show","a supernatural phenomenon","a plane in trouble"],a:0,lines:[["eri","Last Saturday we were camping near Cotopaxi and we saw three orange lights moving slowly in a line. My cousin was convinced they must have been aliens, and for a moment it was pretty spine-tingling, I'll admit. A plane? No way, they were completely silent. Then I remembered there was a festival in Latacunga that night with a light show. They could easily have been drones. That's the most plausible explanation, if you ask me."]]},
{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a news story.",q:"What does the woman say about the story?",o:["She wants to share it with her family.","She has found out that it is false.","She believes it was written by a journalist."],a:1,lines:[["mic","Did you see that thing about the volcano? Apparently scientists have predicted an eruption next week."],["hea","Yeah, my aunt sent it to me this morning. It does look like a real news website."],["mic","I was going to send it to my parents."],["hea","Don't. I looked up the scientist it quotes, and he doesn't exist. The institute has already said nobody predicted anything. It's a fake."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about keeping a gratitude journal.",q:"How does she feel about it now?",o:["She is surprised that it has helped her.","She thinks it takes up too much time.","She is disappointed with the results."],a:0,lines:[["isa","Honestly, I only started because my sister wouldn't stop going on about it. I was sure it would be one of those silly trends that don't do anything, and I didn't expect to keep it up for more than a week. It only takes five minutes before bed, so it isn't exactly time-consuming. And, to my amazement, I've noticed I'm much calmer these days. Who would have thought writing three lines could make such a difference?"]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about the Inti Raymi festival.",q:"What did he enjoy most?",o:["the traditional food","the dancing in the square","the music in the procession"],a:1,lines:[["dan","I went to Inti Raymi in Otavalo for the first time last June. The food was amazing, a huge feast of corn, potatoes and roast pork, and I ate far too much. There was folk music everywhere too, which was lovely, though after six hours of the same song it gets a bit repetitive! But what I'll remember is the dancing. When they pulled me into the circle in the square, stamping and turning with hundreds of people, I didn't want it to stop."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about her new manager.",q:"What does she think of him?",o:["He is too cautious.","He is arrogant.","He is very organised."],a:2,lines:[["sar","Everyone warned me he'd be grumpy and difficult, but I'm actually really impressed. He's got an amazing eye for detail; he noticed a mistake in a report that three of us had already checked. Some people find him a bit cold, but he's never rude, and he certainly doesn't think he's better than anyone else. And he doesn't hesitate either. If something needs deciding, he decides. Every meeting has a clear plan, and we always finish on time."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about using AI for his university work.",q:"What is his attitude to it?",o:["He uses it, but checks everything it produces.","He refuses to use it at all.","He thinks it will soon replace teachers."],a:0,lines:[["fab","Some of my classmates won't touch AI; they think it's cheating. I don't agree. I use it to explain things I didn't understand in lectures, or to find the bug in my code. But I'd never hand in what it writes without checking. Last month it gave me a formula for a circuit that was completely wrong, and it sounded so confident! People say it'll replace teachers one day, but after that, I'm not so sure."]]}]},
{title:"Set 2",ex:[
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a famous conspiracy theory.",q:"Why does she think people believe it?",o:["The evidence is hard to understand.","They enjoy the idea of a secret.","They don't trust scientists."],a:1,lines:[["bel","My uncle is pretty convinced that the Moon landings were filmed in a studio. I've shown him all the proof, the rocks, the photos, the mirrors they left up there, and he doesn't argue, he just smiles. People assume it's about not trusting scientists, but he's an engineer, he loves science! The way I see it, it's more exciting to believe you know a secret that the rest of the world has missed. The truth is just less fun."]]},
{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a password.",q:"What does the man advise the woman to do?",o:["write the password down somewhere safe","use the same password for every account","change her password immediately"],a:2,lines:[["ali","I got an email saying someone tried to log in to my bank account from another country. Weird, isn't it?"],["lew","That's not weird, that's serious. What's your password?"],["ali","I'm not telling you! But, well, it's the same one I use for everything."],["lew","Then you'd better change it right now, before you do anything else. And use a different one for each account. You can write them down later if you need to."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about a decision he made in the past.",q:"What does he regret?",o:["choosing the wrong university course","not spending more time with his grandfather","moving away from his home town"],a:1,lines:[["ada","When I was twenty, I moved from Riobamba to Quito to study engineering, and I've never regretted that; it was the best decision I ever made. But I was so taken up with my studies that I hardly ever went home. My grandfather used to call and ask when I was coming, and I always said next month. He died during my final year. If only I'd gone back more often. I'd give anything for one more afternoon with him."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about Carnaval in Guaranda.",q:"What surprised her about the festival?",o:["how friendly strangers were","how cold the weather was","how much water was thrown"],a:0,lines:[["emm","I knew about the water fights, of course, and the foam and the coloured powder, so I'd brought a raincoat. And everyone had told me Guaranda would be freezing, which it was. What I hadn't expected was the welcome. Families I'd never met invited me into their homes for a drink and a plate of food, and an old man taught me the coplas they sing. By the end, I felt like part of the town."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about his spending habits.",q:"What is he doing?",o:["explaining why he can't stick to a budget","describing how he has started saving money","complaining about the cost of living"],a:1,lines:[["mic","I used to be terrible with money. Everything went on takeaways and stuff I didn't need. Then a friend showed me this simple trick: as soon as I get paid, a fixed amount goes into a separate account that I can't touch. Sure, things are more expensive than a few years ago, but that's not the point. It's been eight months, and for the first time in my life I've actually got something put aside for emergencies."]]},
{ctx:"You hear two colleagues talking about a personality test.",q:"What do they agree about?",o:["The results were accurate.","The test was too long.","Tests like this can be useful."],a:2,lines:[["nic","So, what did the test say about you?"],["eri","Apparently I'm not very agreeable. I don't think that's true at all!"],["nic","Hmm, mine said I'm really outgoing, which, well, is spot on. It was quick too, only ten minutes."],["eri","Fair enough. And I suppose it made me think about how I come across in meetings, which is no bad thing."],["nic","Exactly. Even if they're not perfect, they make you reflect."]]}]},
{title:"Set 3",ex:[
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about a ghost story.",q:"What does she say about the story?",o:["It has been debunked by experts.","She can't explain what happened.","Her grandmother invented it."],a:1,lines:[["lil","My grandmother used to tell this story about the old house next to hers in Cuenca. Every night at midnight, you'd hear a piano playing, even though nobody had lived there for years. I always thought she'd made it up to scare us. Then one summer I stayed with her, and I heard it myself. I went over the next morning; there wasn't even a piano inside. I'm left scratching my head to this day."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about social media.",q:"What is his main point?",o:["Social media is designed to keep us using it.","Young people use social media too much.","Social media is good for staying informed."],a:0,lines:[["dan","People say teenagers are addicted to their phones, but adults are just as bad, believe me. And it isn't a question of willpower. Every notification, every like gives your brain a small rush, and the apps are built by very clever people to make sure you keep coming back. The newsfeed never ends, on purpose. Yes, you can find useful information, but that's not what these platforms were designed for. They were designed to hold your attention."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a woman describing her brother.",q:"What does she say about him?",o:["He has become more cautious with age.","He makes decisions too quickly.","He prefers to be on his own."],a:0,lines:[["hea","When we were kids, my brother was completely happy-go-lucky. He'd jump off rocks into rivers without checking how deep they were and spend all his money the day he got it. Now he's thirty-five, with two children, and he's a different person. He reads every contract twice, he never makes a snap decision, and he worries if the kids are five minutes late. He's still really sociable, though. He loves having people round."]]},
{ctx:"You hear two friends talking about a festival.",q:"Why is the man disappointed?",o:["The fireworks display was cancelled.","The parade was too crowded.","He missed the procession."],a:2,lines:[["ali","Did you enjoy the fiestas? The fireworks were incredible, weren't they?"],["ada","They were, and the parade was great fun, even with all the people. But I really wanted to see the religious procession on Sunday morning. My grandmother used to take part every year."],["ali","Oh no, what happened?"],["ada","I overslept! By the time I got to the church, it had finished."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a man talking about inequality.",q:"What is his opinion?",o:["Poverty has decreased because of globalization.","Talent alone does not decide people's chances.","Rich countries should donate more money."],a:1,lines:[["lew","I grew up in a village where only two of us went to university, and people love to say we made it because we were the clever ones. That's nonsense. Half my classmates were brighter than me. The difference was that my parents could afford a bus ticket to the city and a few books. Of course globalization has created wealth, and donations help, but until opportunities are fairer, a child's future depends more on luck than on talent."]]},
{ctx:"You hear a woman talking about taking cold showers.",q:"Why did she stop?",o:["She found it too unpleasant.","She didn't notice any benefits.","It made her ill."],a:1,lines:[["sar","I tried the cold shower thing for a whole month. Everyone online said I'd have loads more energy and feel amazing. And honestly, after the first few days, the cold wasn't that bad; I got used to it. I didn't get sick or anything either. But I felt exactly the same as before. Not happier, not more awake, nothing. So I thought, what's the point? I'm back to warm showers, and I'm very content."]]}]}
];

const CL_B23_2 = [
{title:"A talk about Inti Raymi",intro:"You will hear a guide called Rosa Cachiguango giving a talk to visitors about the Inti Raymi festival in Otavalo.",voice:"ali",
text:"Good evening, everyone, and welcome to Otavalo. My name is Rosa Cachiguango, and tonight I'd like to tell you a little about Inti Raymi, which you'll be able to see for yourselves over the next few days.\nInti Raymi takes place around the June solstice, the time of year when the sun is at its highest point in the sky. In Kichwa, the name means 'festival of the sun'. For the Indigenous communities of the Andes, it's the most important celebration of the year, and its origin goes back long before the Spanish arrived.\nThe festival celebrates the harvest of maize and other crops, and it's a way of thanking the sun and the earth for everything they give us. It's also a time to remember our ancestors, who celebrated in exactly the same places.\nThe celebrations begin with a ritual bath. At midnight, before the main days, many people bathe in the Peguche waterfall, just outside town. The water is very cold, but it's believed to purify the body and give people strength for the dancing ahead.\nAnd there is a lot of dancing! Groups of dancers move in a circle, stamping hard on the ground. We say this is to wake up Pachamama, Mother Earth. Listen out for the sound of the flutes and the bells the dancers wear on their legs.\nOne character you will definitely notice is the aya uma. He wears a colourful mask with two faces, which symbolize day and night, and he leads the dancers through the streets.\nIn some communities, groups of dancers 'take' the main square of the church in San Juan. This used to be quite violent, with fights between different communities, but nowadays it's much more peaceful.\nA few practical tips. Please remember that this is a celebration for the community, not a show for tourists. You are very welcome to watch and even to join the circle if you're invited, but please ask permission before taking photos of people, especially the elders.\nThe celebrations can go on for about two weeks, so you don't need to see everything in one day. And because you will be walking and standing for hours, on stone streets, I strongly recommend wearing comfortable shoes.\nFinally, if someone offers you a drink or a plate of food, it's polite to accept, even a little. Sharing is at the heart of Inti Raymi.\nThank you, and enjoy the festival.",
items:[["Inti Raymi takes place around the June ___.",["solstice"]],["In Kichwa, the name means 'festival of the ___'.",["sun"]],["The festival celebrates the ___ of maize and other crops.",["harvest"]],["At midnight, many people bathe in the Peguche ___.",["waterfall"]],["Dancers stamp on the ground to wake up ___.",["Pachamama","Mother Earth"]],["The two faces of the aya uma's mask symbolize day and ___.",["night"]],["Taking the square in San Juan used to be quite ___.",["violent"]],["Visitors should ask permission before taking ___ of people.",["photos","photographs"]],["The celebrations can go on for about ___.",["two weeks","2 weeks"]],["Rosa recommends wearing comfortable ___.",["shoes"]]]},
{title:"A presentation about the Cottingley fairies",intro:"You will hear a student called Daniel giving a presentation about a famous mystery, the Cottingley fairies.",voice:"dan",
text:"Good morning, everyone. Today I'm going to talk about one of the most famous photo mysteries in history: the Cottingley fairies.\nIt all began in the summer of 1917, in a small village called Cottingley, in the north of England. Two cousins, Elsie Wright and Frances Griffiths, used to play by a stream at the bottom of the garden. Elsie was sixteen and Frances was nine. They kept telling their parents that they had seen fairies there, and of course nobody believed them. So one day, they borrowed Elsie's father's camera to prove it.\nWhen he developed the photo, it showed Frances sitting behind a group of tiny dancing fairies with wings. A few weeks later they took a second photo, this time of Elsie with a small creature called a gnome. Elsie's father thought the fairies were simply made of paper, and he refused to lend them his camera again.\nThat might have been the end of the story. However, in 1919, Elsie's mother showed the photos at a meeting, and soon they were published. They captured the public's imagination. It was just after the First World War, and many people wanted to believe in something magical.\nOne of those people was the writer Arthur Conan Doyle, the creator of Sherlock Holmes. Ironically, the man who invented the most logical detective in literature was absolutely convinced that the fairies were real, and he wrote an article about them in 1920.\nWere the photos ever verified? Well, experts at the camera company Kodak examined them. They found no sign that the photos had been faked, but they refused to confirm that they were genuine either.\nSo what really happened? It wasn't until 1983 that the cousins, by then old women, finally admitted the truth. Elsie had copied the fairies from a children's book, drawn them on card and held them up with hat pins. They said they had been too embarrassed to confess earlier, especially after such a famous man had believed them.\nHowever, there's one final twist. Frances always insisted that the fifth photo was real, and that she really had seen fairies by the stream. Elsie said they were all fakes. Nobody knows for certain which of them was telling the truth.\nToday, some of the photos and one of the cameras are kept in a museum in Bradford.\nIn a nutshell, the Cottingley fairies show how easily we fall for something when we want it to be true. Thank you for listening.",
items:[["The first photo was taken in the summer of ___.",["1917"]],["When the story began, Frances was ___ years old.",["nine","9"]],["Elsie's father thought the fairies were made of ___.",["paper"]],["After they were published, the photos captured the public's ___.",["imagination"]],["Arthur Conan Doyle was the creator of ___.",["Sherlock Holmes"]],["Experts at Kodak refused to confirm that the photos were ___.",["genuine"]],["The cousins admitted the truth in ___.",["1983"]],["Elsie had copied the fairies from a children's ___.",["book"]],["Frances always said that the ___ photo was real.",["fifth","5th"]],["Some of the photos are now kept in a ___ in Bradford.",["museum"]]]},
{title:"A talk about online safety",intro:"You will hear an IT officer called Megan Ross giving a talk to first-year university students about staying safe online.",voice:"bel",
text:"Hi, everyone, and welcome to university. I know this isn't the most exciting talk of your first week, but bear with me, because what I'm going to tell you could save you a lot of hassle.\nLet's start with passwords. Most of you probably use the same one everywhere, and probably something like your pet's name and your birthday. If you do, you'd better change it today. My advice is to use a passphrase instead of a password: three or four random words together, like 'purple volcano coffee train'. It's long, so it's hard to crack, but it's easy to remember.\nSecondly, turn on two-step verification for your email and your bank. It means that even if someone steals your password, they still need a code from your phone to get in.\nNow, most attacks on students don't involve clever hacking at all. They begin with a fake email. It might look as if it's from the university, telling you that your account will be closed unless you click a link. Be on your guard. Check the sender's address carefully, and if in doubt, don't click.\nThe same goes for messages that seem to come from your bank. Remember, a bank will never ask for your PIN by message or by phone. Never.\nBe careful with public wifi too. Connecting in cafés is convenient, but other people on the same network may be able to see what you're doing. Avoid doing your online banking there.\nFor private conversations, choose messaging apps with end-to-end encryption, so that only you and the person you're talking to can read the messages.\nIt's also a good idea to check your privacy settings every few months, because the platforms change them more often than you'd think. And think twice before you share. Lots of people post their holiday photos while they're still away, which tells everyone that their flat is empty.\nIf something does go wrong, if you think your account has been hacked or you've clicked on something suspicious, don't be embarrassed. Contact the IT help desk straight away. The sooner we know, the more we can do.\nFinally, back up your important files at least once a week. Nobody ever expects their laptop to be stolen the night before a deadline, but it happens every single year.\nRight, any questions?",
items:[["Megan suggests using a ___ instead of a password.",["passphrase"]],["Students should turn on two-step ___.",["verification"]],["Most attacks on students begin with a fake ___.",["email","e-mail"]],["A bank will never ask for your ___ by message or by phone.",["PIN"]],["Students should avoid online banking on public wifi in ___.",["cafés","cafes"]],["Messaging apps should have end-to-end ___.",["encryption"]],["Check your privacy ___ every few months.",["settings"]],["Many people post their ___ while they are still away.",["holiday photos"]],["If something goes wrong, contact the ___ straight away.",["IT help desk","help desk"]],["Back up important files at least once a ___.",["week"]]]},
{title:"A talk about making a budget",intro:"You will hear a financial adviser called Andrés Maldonado giving a talk at a community centre in Cuenca about how to make a budget.",voice:"mic",
text:"Good evening, and thank you all for coming. My name's Andrés Maldonado, and tonight we're going to talk about something most people avoid thinking about: budgets.\nFirst of all, a budget isn't a punishment. It's simply a plan for your money, so that you decide where it goes, instead of wondering at the end of the month where it went.\nA simple way to start is to divide your income into three parts. Half of it should go on needs, like rent, food and transport. Up to thirty percent can be spent on wants: eating out, clothes, going to the cinema. And the remaining twenty percent goes into savings or towards paying off any debt you have.\nThe first thing to build with that twenty percent is an emergency fund. Ideally, you should have three months' expenses put aside, in case you lose your job or have an unexpected bill. It sounds like a lot, and it takes time, but even a small fund will relieve a lot of stress.\nNow let's talk about everyday spending, because that's where most budgets fall apart. My first tip is to always go shopping with a list, and stick to it. Supermarkets are designed to make you buy things you hadn't planned to. And whatever you do, avoid going to the supermarket when you're hungry. You'll come home with three packets of biscuits and no vegetables.\nFor bigger purchases, try the forty-eight hour rule. If you see something expensive that you want, wait forty-eight hours before buying it. You'll be surprised how often the desire simply disappears.\nIt's also worth comparing prices. Fruit and vegetables at the local market are often much cheaper than in the supermarket, and fresher too.\nFor those of you who share a flat, money can cause a lot of arguments. Talk openly with your flatmates about shared costs like electricity and internet from the very beginning, and agree how you'll divide them.\nFinally, a budget isn't something you write once and forget. Review your budget at the end of every month. See what worked, see where you blew it, and adjust. Nobody gets it perfect the first time.\nRight, I've brought some printed budget sheets for you to take home. Let's look at one together.",
items:[["Half of your income should go on ___.",["needs"]],["Up to thirty percent can be spent on ___.",["wants"]],["The remaining twenty percent goes into ___ or paying off debt.",["savings"]],["An emergency fund should cover three months' ___.",["expenses"]],["Always go shopping with a ___.",["list"]],["Avoid going to the supermarket when you're ___.",["hungry"]],["Wait ___ hours before buying something expensive.",["forty-eight","48"]],["Fruit and vegetables at the local ___ are often cheaper.",["market"]],["People who share a flat should talk openly with their ___.",["flatmates"]],["Review your budget at the end of every ___.",["month"]]]}
];

const CL_B23_4 = [
{title:"Interview with a personality psychologist",intro:"You will hear an interview with Dr Marco Vallejo, a psychologist who studies personality at a university in Quito.",voices:{I:V_.emm,G:V_.ada},
script:[["I","My guest today is Dr Marco Vallejo, a psychologist who has spent fifteen years researching personality. Marco, welcome."],["G","Thank you, it's great to be here."],["I","Let's start with the basics. When psychologists talk about personality, what do they actually mean?"],["G","Well, most researchers today use a model called the Big Five. The idea is that you can describe almost anyone using five main traits: openness to new experiences, conscientiousness, which is how organised and careful you are, extroversion, agreeableness and neuroticism, which basically means how easily you get worried or upset. The important thing is that each trait is a scale. Nobody is simply an introvert or an extrovert; we're all somewhere in between."],["I","Many people love horoscopes and the Chinese zodiac. Is there anything in them?"],["G","I get asked that a lot! People expect me to be really rude about them, and I'm not, because I can see why they're fun. But when researchers have tested them, there's no proof at all that the month or year you were born affects your personality. What I find interesting is why people believe them. The descriptions are so general that almost everyone recognises themselves."],["I","Can our personality change, or are we stuck with it?"],["G","For a long time it was believed that personality was fixed by the age of thirty. Now we know that's not true. It does change, slowly, throughout our lives. And the good news is that, on average, it improves. Most people become more conscientious, more agreeable and more emotionally stable as they get older. We don't really know why. It may be that life experiences, like having a job or children, push us in that direction."],["I","So, if I'd had a different childhood, would I have a different personality now?"],["G","Partly, yes, but probably less than you'd think. Studies of twins suggest that around half of the differences between people come from our genes. Parents often feel guilty, thinking they've made their child shy or anxious, and of course the way children are brought up matters. But the friends we choose as teenagers seem to have at least as much influence as our parents do."],["I","Which trait is the most useful to have?"],["G","Everyone hopes I'll say extroversion, because outgoing people seem to have such fun. And it's true they tend to report feeling happier. But if you look at success at work, at university, even at staying healthy, the trait that predicts it best is conscientiousness. People who are persistent and have an eye for detail simply get things done."],["I","And finally, are personality tests useful for employers?"],["G","They can be, if they're scientific. The problem is that many companies use quick online quizzes that have never been tested. I'd say a test can start a useful conversation, but nobody should ever be hired or rejected because of a test alone. People are far too complex for that."],["I","Marco Vallejo, thank you."]],
q:[["What does Marco say about the Big Five traits?","Most people are either introverts or extroverts.","Each trait is measured on a scale.","They describe only a few types of people.",1],["What is Marco's opinion of horoscopes?","He thinks they are rude to people who believe them.","There is no evidence that they describe personality.","They are useful for understanding ourselves.",1],["According to Marco, how does personality usually change with age?","It becomes fixed by the age of thirty.","It becomes more positive over time.","It changes most when people have children.",1],["What does Marco say about the influence of parents?","It is smaller than many people believe.","It is the main cause of shyness.","It is greater than the influence of genes.",0],["What does Marco say about outgoing people?","They tend to say they feel happier.","They are usually more successful at work.","They find it harder to stay healthy.",0],["Which trait best predicts success, according to Marco?","extroversion","agreeableness","conscientiousness",2],["What does Marco think about personality tests at work?","They should be used to decide who gets a job.","They can be useful, but should not be the only factor.","Online quizzes are usually reliable.",1]]},
{title:"Interview with a musician who uses AI",intro:"You will hear an interview with Paula Ríos, a musician and producer from Guayaquil who uses artificial intelligence in her work.",voices:{I:V_.eri,G:V_.lil},
script:[["I","Joining me today is Paula Ríos, a musician and producer whose latest album was made partly with the help of artificial intelligence. Paula, welcome."],["G","Thanks for having me."],["I","How did you start using AI in your music?"],["G","Honestly, I was completely against it at first. I thought it would put musicians out of work, and I still have some concerns about that. But two years ago I was working on a song and I was totally stuck. A friend who's a programmer suggested I try an AI tool, just for fun, to give me some ideas. It produced about fifty short melodies in a couple of minutes. Forty-nine of them were terrible! But one had something interesting, and I ended up building the whole song around it."],["I","So what does the AI actually do on the album?"],["G","People assume it writes the songs, and that's not the case at all. I write all the lyrics myself, and every instrument you hear is played by real musicians. What I use it for is mainly the boring, time-consuming parts of the process, things like cleaning up recordings or trying out different mixes. And sometimes, like with that first song, I use it when I'm out of ideas. It assists me; it doesn't replace me."],["I","Some people say AI music has no soul. What would you say to them?"],["G","I'd say they've got a point. When I listen to a song made entirely by AI, it's technically perfect, but it leaves me cold. It sounds like music, but it isn't trying to say anything. On the other hand, a guitar is just a tool too, and nobody says guitars have no soul. What matters is the person behind the tool and what they want to express."],["I","Has using AI changed the way you work with other musicians?"],["G","I was worried it would, but actually we collaborate more than before. Because I save so much time on the technical side, I can spend more time in the studio with my band, just playing together. That's where the real magic happens."],["I","What worries you most about AI in the music industry?"],["G","People often think I'll say it's the quality, but that's improving every month. It isn't really about fans either; I think they'll always want real artists. What really concerns me is that companies are training these tools on musicians' recordings without asking them or paying them. My own songs were probably used, and nobody asked me. I think there's an urgent need for regulation."],["I","Finally, what advice would you give young musicians?"],["G","Don't be afraid of the technology, but don't let it do the thinking for you. Learn to play an instrument properly first. If you understand music, AI can be a brilliant assistant. If you don't, you'll just end up with the same songs as everyone else."],["I","Paula Ríos, thank you."]],
q:[["How did Paula first feel about using AI?","She was excited about the possibilities.","She was opposed to it.","She thought it would be too difficult.",1],["Why did Paula first try an AI tool?","She couldn't finish a song.","Her record company asked her to.","She wanted to replace some musicians.",0],["What does Paula mainly use AI for?","writing lyrics","playing instruments she can't play","technical tasks that take a long time",2],["What is Paula's opinion of music made entirely by AI?","It is technically poor.","It lacks emotion.","It is as good as music made by people.",1],["How has AI affected Paula's work with her band?","They spend more time playing together.","They need fewer musicians in the studio.","They argue more about the music.",0],["What concerns Paula most about AI in the music industry?","Fans will stop listening to real artists.","The quality of AI music will keep improving.","Musicians' work is being used without permission.",2],["What advice does Paula give young musicians?","avoid using AI completely","learn music properly before relying on AI","use AI to write songs like everyone else",1]]},
{title:"Interview with a long-distance cyclist",intro:"You will hear an interview with Daniel Guerrero, who cycled from Quito to Ushuaia, in the south of Argentina.",voices:{I:V_.bel,G:V_.dan},
script:[["I","My guest today is Daniel Guerrero, who recently cycled more than nine thousand kilometres from Quito to Ushuaia, at the southern tip of Argentina. Daniel, welcome."],["G","Thanks. It's nice to be sitting on a chair instead of a bike!"],["I","What made you decide to do it?"],["G","People usually assume I'm a professional athlete, but I'd never cycled more than fifty kilometres in my life. I was working as an electrical engineer in an office in Quito, and I was fortunate, I had a good salary and a stable job. But I wasn't happy. The turning point was my thirtieth birthday. A friend asked me what I'd do if I knew I couldn't fail, and without thinking, I said 'cycle across South America'. I couldn't stop thinking about it after that."],["I","How did your family react?"],["G","My dad thought I'd gone mad, and my mum was terrified. My sister was the only one who supported me from the start, and she helped me plan the route. Funnily enough, by the end, my dad was the one following my progress most closely. He used to check the map every morning to see where I was."],["I","What was the most difficult part of the journey?"],["G","Everyone expects me to say the mountains in Peru, and they were hard, but you go slowly and you get there. The cold in Patagonia was awful too. But the hardest thing was the wind in the south of Argentina. Some days it was so strong that I could only cycle twenty kilometres. A few times I just sat by the road and cried. I seriously thought about giving up, but I'd told so many people that I was doing it that I couldn't go home."],["I","What kept you going?"],["G","Mostly the people I met. I was going to camp most nights, but in the end I hardly used my tent, because families kept inviting me to stay in their homes. A farmer in Bolivia gave me his own bed and slept on the floor. That kind of generosity is something I'll never forget, and it made me want to persevere."],["I","Has the trip changed you?"],["G","Completely. Before, I was always worried about the future. Now I've learnt that I'm more resilient than I thought, and I savour small things, a hot shower, a cup of coffee, much more. I don't regret leaving my job at all. If I hadn't done the trip, I'd still be sitting in that office, wondering what might have been."],["I","So what's next?"],["G","Lots of people ask if I'm going to cycle across Africa next. Maybe one day! But right now I'm writing a book about the journey, and I'm going to use the money to help a cycling project for teenagers in Quito."],["I","Daniel Guerrero, thank you."]],
q:[["What was Daniel's situation before the trip?","He was a professional cyclist.","He was unhappy in his job.","He was worried about losing his job.",1],["Who supported Daniel's plan from the beginning?","his father","his mother","his sister",2],["What was the hardest part of the journey for Daniel?","the mountains in Peru","the wind in Argentina","the cold in Patagonia",1],["Why didn't Daniel give up?","He didn't want to disappoint the people he had told.","His father persuaded him to continue.","The weather suddenly improved.",0],["Where did Daniel usually sleep during the trip?","in his tent","in cheap hotels","in local people's homes",2],["How has the trip changed Daniel?","He appreciates simple things more.","He regrets leaving his job.","He worries more about the future.",0],["What is Daniel doing now?","cycling across Africa","writing a book","going back to engineering",1]]}
];
